WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.010
- So before I get into that,

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:02.843
let's talk a little bit

00:00:02.843 --> 00:00:04.400
about what the fuck is machine learning?

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.820
'cause it's something that
I think a lot of people

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:06.680
don't really understand.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.040
I'm sure in this crew,

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.990
almost all of you
understand it in some way

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:13.610
but you have to take the remarks

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:14.490
that I've prepared here to tonight

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.613
and so I'm just gonna go
ahead and walk through this.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.540
The way I have been
explaining it to people

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.840
and I'll be curious tonight
to have anybody here sort

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:22.920
of help me refine this.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.200
I'm trying to get better at it.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.320
Is you have a big amount of messy data.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.153
You don't know what to do with it,

00:00:27.153 --> 00:00:28.440
you don't wanna pay
people to go through it,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.730
you don't wanna sit yourself

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.070
and go through it.

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:33.130
So instead, you find comparable data,

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:38.130
training data that in some way
has either direct application

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.460
to the data you're looking at,

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.140
it's like a tiny subset of it

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.348
or it's close enough of a parallel

00:00:44.348 --> 00:00:46.980
that it might be useful in some way.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.540
You use that to then
create basically examples

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:51.690
of right and wrong answers

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.600
that you can give to an algorithm

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.030
and then using those
right and wrong examples,

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.870
instead of just straight
coding instructions,

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.270
you can then turn that algorithm loose

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:02.940
to apply the rules it has learned

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.420
on the messy data that you started with.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.020
So here's an example
of how that might work.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:09.660
Let's say you wanna ask an algorithm,

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.830
okay, algorithm, tell me which is which.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:15.550
Which is a cow and which is a dog?

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:16.930
Tell me the difference
between these two things

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.243
in a bunch of photographs
I'm about to give you?

00:01:19.243 --> 00:01:21.850
Or in some other piece,

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.750
some data that's full of cows and dogs.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.120
Tell me the difference
between these two things.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.380
If you give the system enough cows

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.240
and tell it, these are
correct pictures of cows

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.620
and you give it enough pictures of dogs

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.140
and say these are actually dogs,

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:36.670
you can, in fact, teach it enough examples

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.450
that it can draw the
distinction between the two.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.340
The AI uses patterns to label the data.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.620
That's really all it comes down to.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:48.080
So when you think about it
in terms of just a system

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.650
that just is trying to draw a distinction

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.190
between categories, you
really begin to understand

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.250
that it doesn't actually know anything

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:59.590
beyond the basic, barest minimum it needs

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.140
to distinguish between those two things.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.340
So let's say that in a
bunch of data you've got

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.940
on cows and dogs, there are
two variables you can look at.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.930
Their size and their slobber,
right? (audience chuckling)

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:10.150
It picks those out.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.330
Says here's one that's really consistent

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.670
in the examples you've given me,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.500
and out of that, it can begin charting,

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.440
oh the dogs fall into the slobber category

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:19.950
and they're a little smaller in size.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.270
Here's the cows, they're bigger.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.400
They slobber some but not too much.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.960
And out of that, you can draw this line

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.760
that distinguishes between the two.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.230
This one is dogs, sorry, this one is cows,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.010
this side dogs.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:36.530
You're just teaching it how to
distinguish between the two.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:39.545
People think that, I think
sort of the misperception

00:02:39.545 --> 00:02:43.130
among the many, many
people that I talk to tends

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:46.130
to be that there's something
more sophisticated going on

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:49.126
in this but really, it is just
describing the difference.

00:02:49.126 --> 00:02:51.440
Machine learning really
just uses the patterns

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.150
to just draw that distinguishing line.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.230
That's really all it comes down to.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.643
So I think for me what's
so important to understand

00:02:57.643 --> 00:02:59.450
is that it doesn't really know anything

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:03.700
about dogs or cows, it just
knows the simplest possible way

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.295
to tell the difference based
on the data you've given it.

00:03:06.295 --> 00:03:08.878
(bell ringing)

